\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Transport of the retained Schur map}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Over a commutative unital ring #{k}, give #{M=X\oplus Y} and
#{M'=X'\oplus Y'} [chosen splittings](fdip-001T).
Suppose isomorphisms #{P:M\to M'}, #{P_X:X\to X'},
#{P_Y:Y\to Y'} preserve the inclusions and projections:
##{\begin{aligned}
P\iota_X&=\iota'_XP_X,&P\iota_Y&=\iota'_YP_Y,\\
\pi'_XP&=P_X\pi_X,&\pi'_YP&=P_Y\pi_Y.
\end{aligned}}
Let #{Q'=PQP^{-1}}. Write its four split components and those of
#{Q} as #{A:X\to X}, #{B:Y\to X}, #{E:X\to Y}, #{C:Y\to Y},
and their primed versions. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
A'&=P_XAP_X^{-1},&B'&=P_XBP_Y^{-1},\\
E'&=P_YEP_X^{-1},&C'&=P_YCP_Y^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
If #{C} is invertible, the [split Schur maps](fdip-001T) obey
##{S'=P_XSP_X^{-1},\qquad S=A-BC^{-1}E.}
For #{K=C^{-1}E:X\to Y}, #{K'=(C')^{-1}E'},
##{K'P_X=P_YK,\qquad -K'(P_Xx)=P_Y(-Kx).}
Thus the [eliminated stationary field](fdip-001W) #{y_*(x)=-Kx} is transported
with its sign intact.}

\proof{
\p{For indices #{i,j\in\{X,Y\}}, the inclusion and projection
identities give
##{\pi'_iQ'\iota'_j
 =P_i(\pi_iQ\iota_j)P_j^{-1}.}
Taking #{(i,j)=(X,X),(X,Y),(Y,X),(Y,Y)} gives all four typed
identities. Inverting the last one gives
#{(C')^{-1}=P_YC^{-1}P_Y^{-1}}. Substitution cancels each adjacent
#{P_Y^{-1}P_Y} in #{B'(C')^{-1}E'}, proving the Schur formula.
The same substitution in #{K'} proves #{K'P_X=P_YK} and hence the
stationary-field identity.}
}

\p{When #{Q} is also invertible, the
[inverse-compression theorem](fdip-001V) says
##{S^{-1}=\pi_XQ^{-1}\iota_X.}
Conjugating and using the split maps gives
##{(S')^{-1}=P_XS^{-1}P_X^{-1}
             =\pi'_X(Q')^{-1}\iota'_X.}
This uses an actual inverse. No pairing has been assumed; only for an
orthogonal split and self-adjoint #{Q} does one further have
#{E=B^\dagger}, by \ref{fdip-001X}.}

\p{For a vertex bijection #{p:V\to V'}, let #{R'=pR},
#{X=k^R}, #{X'=k^{R'}}, and let #{P_R} be inverse precomposition
by #{p|_R}. At #{p(v)}, #{v\in R}, both sides of
##{\pi_{R'}P=P_R\pi_R}
take the value #{f(v)}. The target space is #{k^{pR}}, even if the
labels of #{R} and #{pR} differ. This equation supplies the moving
projection square in [Gaussian marginal transport](fdip-000H).}

\p{If a group action preserves #{R}, its restrictions compose on
#{k^R} and the square becomes #{\pi_RP_g=P_{g,R}\pi_R}.
Faithfulness of the full action need not survive restriction: a
permutation moving only eliminated vertices acts trivially on retained
fields. The [opposite-pair half-turn](fdip-000K) has a separate
nontrivial retained action.}

\p{Stable subsets and equivariance follow the convention of
\citet{p. 58}{milne2025group}. The real Schur convention is
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}, with the
source's first pivot exchanged to the eliminated #{C} block.
The typed transport proof above uses only the chosen splitting, and
does not by itself supply any integral or Gaussian normalizer.}
